            <ArticleTitle>Atlantooccipital Rotatory Dislocation: A Case Report and Systematic Review.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1878-8750(17)31924-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.wneu.2017.11.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Atlanto-occipital rotatory dislocation (AORD) has been rarely reported in the literature; for this reason, the clinical-radiological characteristics of this injury are not well described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The authors describe the case of a 67 years old male who sustained a cervical spine trauma. He only referred neck pain and was neurologically intact. The CT scan showed a rotatory displacement of the atlanto-occipital joints (AOJs) associated with a widened condylar-C1 interval; in addition, the MRI revealed injuries to the stabilizing ligaments of this area. A systematic literature review was also performed to identify previous cases of patients with AORD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The patient was treated with craniocervical fixation from occipital to C1 achieving a good outcome. The literature review yielded 9 cases of patients with AORD. In comparison to cases of patients with atlanto-occipital dislocation (AOD), patients with rotatory dislocations have less severe degree of displacement of the AOJs and better clinical outcome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Compared to previously classified atlanto-occipital dislocations, atlanto-occipital rotatory dislocation is an independent and unique variation. AORD presents with different biomechanical, clinical-radiologic and prognostic characteristics that represents an important addition to the spectrum of AOD injuries.</AbstractText>
